Troubleshooting
Issue 1: Tests fail intermittently with "AssertionError" on `assertIsSatisfied()" Symptom: A test passes sometimes and fails other times with no code changes. Diagnostic hint: check whether the mock endpoint is being reused across tests without being reset, and whether the default assertion timeout is too short for the message volume being sent.

Issue 2: Maven cannot resolve camel-test-junit5 or reports NoClassDefFoundError for Camel classes Symptom: The build fails during mvn test with dependency or class-loading errors. Diagnostic hint: confirm the camel.version property matches across all camel-* dependencies in pom.xml, and run mvn dependency:tree to look for version conflicts.

Conclusion
In this lab, you built a complete Apache Camel testing workflow from scratch: a Maven project with a multi-route order processing application, and a full suite of JUnit 5 tests built on CamelTestSupport. You learned how to inject and reset MockEndpoint instances, simulate a range of realistic inputs including missing and invalid data, and validate both message bodies and headers produced by routes and custom processors. Along the way you practiced core testing best practices for integration code: isolating routes from external systems using mocks, asserting on both content and metadata, and using timeouts to handle asynchronous message delivery reliably. From here, natural next steps include exploring Camel's performance and load testing tools such as camel-test-spring with concurrent producers, integrating these same route definitions with a real message broker like ActiveMQ or Kafka for end-to-end testing, and adding these test classes to a continuous integration pipeline so route regressions are caught automatically on every commit.
